== 2. Painting Sin with Virtue’s Colors

The second device of Satan to draw the soul to sin is to paint sin with virtue’s colors. Satan knows that if he presented sin in its own nature and dress, the soul would rather flee from it than yield to it. Therefore he presents it to us, not in its own proper colors, but painted and gilded over with the name and show of virtue, so that we may the more easily be overcome by it, and take the more pleasure in committing it. Pride he presents to the soul under the name and notion of neatness and cleanliness; covetousness (which the apostle condemns as idolatry) as nothing but good thrift; drunkenness as good fellowship; riotous living under the name and notion of generosity; and wantonness as a passing folly of youth, #emph[etc.]

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are these.]

First, consider that sin is not one bit less filthy, vile and abominable for being colored and painted with virtue’s colors. A poisonous pill is not one bit less poisonous because it is gilded over with gold. A wolf is not one bit less a wolf because he has put on a sheep’s skin. The devil is not one bit less a devil because he sometimes appears like an angel of light. So neither is sin any bit less filthy and abominable for being painted over with virtue’s colors.

The second remedy is this: the more sin is painted under the color#footnote[#emph[Turpiora sunt vitia quae virtutum specie celantur] \[Vices are the fouler for being hidden under the appearance of virtues\], Jerome. So the Illuminates (as they called themselves), a pestilent sect in Aragon, professing and affecting in themselves a kind of angelic purity, fell suddenly to justifying bestiality, as many have done in these days.] of virtue, the more dangerous it is to the souls of men. We see this plainly in these days, in the very many souls who are turned out of the way that is holy (the way in which their souls have had sweet and glorious communion with God) into ways of the highest vanity and folly, by Satan’s neat coloring over of sin and painting vice under the name and color of virtue. This is so notoriously known that I need only name it. The most dangerous vermin is too often found under the fairest and sweetest flowers; the fairest glove is often drawn on the foulest hand; and the richest robes are often put on the filthiest bodies. So too the fairest and sweetest names are put on the greatest and most horrible vices and errors in the world. Ah, if only we did not have so many sad proofs of this among us!

The third remedy against this device is to look on sin with the eye with#footnote[Tacitus says of Tiberius that when his sins appeared in their own colors, they so terrified and tormented him that he declared to the Senate that he suffered death daily.] which within a few hours we will see it. Ah, souls! When you#super[+] lie on a dying bed and stand before a judgment seat, sin will be unmasked, and its dress and robes will then be taken off. Then it will appear more vile, filthy and terrible than hell itself. Then what once appeared most sweet will appear most bitter; what appeared most beautiful will appear most ugly; and what appeared most delightful will then appear most dreadful to the soul. Ah, the shame, the pain, the gall, the bitterness, the horror, the hell, that the sight of sin will raise in poor souls when its dress is taken off! Sin will surely prove evil and bitter to the soul when its robes are taken off. A man may have the stone though he feels no attack of it. Conscience will work at last, though for the present one may feel no attack of accusation. Laban#footnote[Satan, who now lures you to sin, will before long make you see that (#emph[Peccatum est deicidium]) sin is a murdering of God. And this will make you murder two at once, your soul and your body, unless the Lord in mercy holds your hand.] showed himself for what he was at parting. Sin will be bitterness in the end, when it appears to the soul in its own filthy nature. The devil deals with men as the panther does with beasts: he hides his ugly head until his sweet scent has drawn them into his power. Until we have sinned, Satan is a flatterer; when we have sinned, he is a tyrant. Ah, souls! The day is at hand when the devil will pull off the paint and decoration that he has put on sin, and present that monster sin to your#super[+] souls in such a monstrous shape that it will cause your thoughts to be troubled, your face to change, the joints of your hips to be loosed, your knees to knock one against another, and your hearts to be so terrified that you will be ready, with Ahithophel and Judas, to strangle and hang your bodies on earth and your souls in hell, if the Lord does not have more mercy on you than he had on them. Oh, therefore, look on sin now as you#super[+] must look on it to all eternity, and as God, conscience and Satan will present it to you another day.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that these very sins that Satan paints, and puts new names and colors#footnote[#emph[Una guttula plus valet quam coelum & terra.] Luther; that is, one little drop (speaking of the blood of Christ) is worth more than heaven and earth.] on, cost the best blood, the noblest blood, the life-blood, the heart-blood of the Lord Jesus. Consider that Christ should come from the eternal bosom of his Father to a region of sorrow and death; that God should be manifested in the flesh, the Creator made a creature; that he who was clothed with glory should be wrapped in rags of flesh; that he who filled heaven and earth with his glory should be cradled in a manger; that the power of God should flee from weak man, the God of Israel into Egypt; that the God of the law should be subject to the law;#footnote[One of the rabbis, when he read what bitter torments the Messiah would suffer when he came into the world, cried out, #emph[Veniat Messias et ego non videam]\; that is, “Let the Messiah come, but let me not see him.” Dionysius, being in Egypt at the time of Christ’s suffering, and seeing an eclipse of the sun, and knowing it to be contrary to nature, cried out (#emph[aut Deus naturae patitur aut mundi machina dissolvitur]), “Either the God of nature suffers, or the frame of the world will be dissolved.”] the God of circumcision circumcised; the God who made the heavens working at Joseph’s humble trade; that he who binds the devils in chains should be tempted; that he whose is the world and all its fullness should hunger and thirst; that the God of strength should be weary, the Judge of all flesh condemned, the God of life put to death; that he who is one with his Father should cry out in misery, “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?”; that he who had the keys of hell and death at his belt should lie imprisoned in another man’s tomb, having nowhere in his lifetime to lay his head, nor after death to lay his body. Consider that that head, before which the angels cast down their crowns, should be crowned with thorns; that those eyes, purer than the sun, should be put out by the darkness of death; that those ears, which hear nothing but the hallelujahs of saints and angels, should hear the blasphemies of the multitude; that that face, which was fairer than the sons of men, should be spat on by those beastly, wretched Jews; that that mouth and tongue, which spoke as no man ever spoke, should be accused of blasphemy; that those hands that freely wielded the scepter of heaven should be nailed to the cross; that those feet, like fine brass, should be nailed to the cross for man’s sins; that each sense should be afflicted: his feeling or touch with a spear and nails;#footnote[It is an excellent saying of Bernard (#emph[quanto pro nobis vilior tanto nobis charior]): the more lowly Christ made himself for us, the more dear he ought to be to us.] his smell with stench, being crucified at Golgotha, the place of skulls; his taste with vinegar and gall; his hearing with reproaches; and his sight with his mother and disciples mourning him; his soul comfortless and forsaken; and all this for those very sins that Satan paints and puts fine colors on. Oh, how the consideration of this should stir up the soul against sin, and move the soul to flee from it, and to use all holy means by which sin may be subdued and destroyed! After Julius Caesar was murdered, Antony brought out his coat, all bloody and cut, and laid it before the people, saying, “Look, here you#super[+] have your emperor’s coat, bloody and torn like this.” At this the people were at once in an uproar, and cried out to kill those murderers. They took the tables and stools that were in the place and set them on fire, and ran to the houses of those who had killed Caesar and burned them. So when we consider that sin has killed our Lord Jesus, ah, how it should provoke our hearts to take revenge on sin, which has murdered the Lord of glory and#footnote[#emph[Nolo vivere sine vulnere cum te video vulneratum.] “O my God, as long as I see your wounds, I will never live without a wound,” says Bonaventure.] has done a harm that all the devils in hell could never have done! It was good counsel that one gave: “Never let the thoughts of a crucified Christ go out of your#super[+] minds. Let these be food and drink to you#super[+]; let them be your sweetness and consolation, your honey and your desire, your reading and your meditation, your life, death and resurrection.”
